{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-17810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A builder has $320$ meters of fencing and she wants to enclose a rectangular parking lot. One side next to a building and so this side doesn't need to be fenced. Find $x+y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the parking lot that has the greatest area." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-17810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/275803f7f72469a8427b137b5e8523475cc0605f033bedaa2280a5d28f453c38.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $320\\,\\text{m}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=320.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =320 \\\\ x & =320-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=320-2y$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(320-2y)y \\\\ A & =320y-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(y)=320-4y,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 320-4y & =0 \\\\ 4y & =320 \\\\ y & =\\frac{320}{4} \\\\ y & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $y=80$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(y)=-4\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(80)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(80)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=80$ is indeed a maximum of $A(y).$\n\nFinally, when $y=80$, we have \n\n$$\nx=320-2(80)=160.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=160$ and $y=80,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+y=240.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-17810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-17810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-17810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "200\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "240\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "270\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "280\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "320\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-17810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "240\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A contractor has $18\\,in$ of trim to mark out a rectangular opening. Find the maximum area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that can be marked out with the trim. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,{in}^{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectagular area.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/59c6b4e09015898c1cd77d96fba2bc0dca037ef7462a15f90765257888e3331d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $18.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =18 \\\\ y & =9-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=9-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(9-x) \\\\ A & =9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=9-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 9-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{9}{2})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{9}{2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{9}{2}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=9-\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in$ and $y=\\frac{9}{2}\\,in,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{max}}=xy=\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=20.25\\,{in}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-346240" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346240" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{81}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-3462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A farmer needs to enclose a field with a rectangular fence. He has $500\\,\\text{ft}$ of fencing material. A large building is on one side of the field so this side will not require any fencing. Determine the largest possible area that can be enclosed by the rectangular fence." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-3462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/050111089f76ecb6d2b278fce0d1faa71635bc07d107d2539cf5f4bb28a32476.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $500\\,\\text{ft}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=500.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =500 \\\\ x & =500-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=500-2y$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(500-2y)y \\\\ A & =500y-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(y)=500-4y,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 500-4y & =0 \\\\ 4y & =500 \\\\ y & =\\frac{500}{4} \\\\ y & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $y=125$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(y)=-4\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(125)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(125)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=125$ is indeed a maximum of $A(y).$\n\nFinally, when $y=125$, we have \n\n$$\ny=500-2(125)=250.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=250$ and $y=125,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy=250\\cdot 125=31\\,250.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-3462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-3462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-3462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "21\\,875\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}"] ["math" "31\\,250\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}"] ["math" "56\\,875\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}"] ["math" "62\\,500\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}"] ["math" "6\\,250\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-3462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "31\\,250\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-104195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A gardener wants to fence a rectangular flowerbed with $15$ meters of fencing. To two decimal places, what is the largest area that he can make with the fencing?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-104195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $15.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{15}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(\\frac{15}{2}-x) \\\\ A & =\\frac{15}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{15}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{15}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{15}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{15}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{15}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{15}{2}-\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{15}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy=\\frac{15}{4}\\cdot \\frac{15}{4}≈14.06\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-104195" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-104195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-104195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12.93\\,{m}^{2}"] ["math" "13.72\\,{m}^{2}"] ["math" "14.06\\,{m}^{2}"] ["math" "16.08\\,{m}^{2}"] ["math" "17.35\\,{m}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-104195" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14.06\\,{m}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangle has a perimeter of $11$ units. What is the largest possible area of the rectangle, rounded to two decimal places?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $11.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=11.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =11 \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{11}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(\\frac{11}{2}-x) \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{11}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{11}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{11}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{11}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy={(\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}≈7.56.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-14630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$1.38$ square units"] ["text" "$2.75$ square units"] ["text" "$3.32$ square units"] ["text" "$5.5$ square units"] ["text" "$7.56$ square units"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$7.56$ square units"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangle has an area of $450\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ What is the minimum possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{min}}$ of the rectangle? Do not round your answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/cd9fa71ea72045881c25c80de4f6d8b294b60d92ea4e4f27170d68849ce31431.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $450\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=450.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{450}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{900}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =450 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{1800}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{1800}{(15\\sqrt{2})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{2})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{15\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{30}{\\sqrt{2}}=15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{2}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{2}\\,cm.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{2}=60\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-346249" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346249" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangle has an area of $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ What is the minimum possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{min}}$ of the rectangle? Do not round your answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/cf3d29b794698ff529da4c21f6fd02b3487d1650ec84b3c1f992280616f7701d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=675.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{675}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1350}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1350 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =675 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2700}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{2700}{(15\\sqrt{3})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{15\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{45}{\\sqrt{3}}=15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{3}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{3}\\,ft.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{3}=60\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-346253" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346253" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A rectangle has an area of $12$ square units. What is the minimum possible perimeter of the rectangle, rounded to two decimal places?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/a80e6f5620ba4e77487b675390fa04af778e538ecedbd55c783194cf3ab5772b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $12.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{12}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{24}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{24}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{48}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(2\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{48}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(2\\sqrt{3})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=2\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{12}{2\\sqrt{3}}=2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $y=2\\sqrt{3}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nFinally, the smallest possible perimeter is\n\n$$\nP=4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}≈13.86\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-14637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$13.86$ units"] ["text" "$24$ units"] ["text" "$3$ units"] ["text" "$3.46$ units"] ["text" "$6.93$ units"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-14637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$13.86$ units"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A park wants to build a rectangular skating area of area $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ One side of the area is adjacent to a wall, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the area that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{yd}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/f1ce707b311c320fe7dbf3ba2ad1daff6669a67ab1d7652fc8d98065c9ae747d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $1250\\,{yd}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=1250.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{1250}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{2500}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{2500}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2500 \\\\ x & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=50$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{5000}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(50)=\\frac{5000}{(50)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{25}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(50)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=50$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=50,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1250}{50}=25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=50\\,yd$ and $y=25\\,yd,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=50+25=75\\,yd.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-346256" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346256" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "75"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A farmer wants to build a rectangular livestock pen of area $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ One side of the pen is adjacent to a barn, so that side does not require fencing. Find $x+y$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the pen that minimize the amount of fencing required.\n\n$x+y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/8377f61340020a602e6226932a14fb56ceb803bc9efff05223682f9cca1cf91e.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=x+2y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $648\\,{ft}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=648.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =x+2(\\frac{648}{x}) \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1296}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1296}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1296 \\\\ x & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=36$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2592}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(36)=\\frac{2592}{(36)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{18}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(36)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=36$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{648}{36}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the lot that has the least perimeter are $x=36\\,ft$ and $y=18\\,ft,$ and we conclude that \n\n$$\nx+y=36+18=54\\,ft.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a2b2a33990521042877d08e6bfd40a033d16876de299c4194c7dbc6a13ff409"} {"question" "q-346255" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "q-346255" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "54"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/state.json" "file_sha256" "4e4795972c525d0ee982529627e25f78ab6cb3a50c6c50aede17b66ccbcfe873"} {"question" "e-345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A farmer has $500$ meters of fencing and wants to fence off a rectangular field that borders a straight river. He needs no fence along the river. Find $x+y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the field with the largest area that the farmer can fence off." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/example-345.json" "file_sha256" "d980a6047802007fac8163111e2d1e384673cbf2640e73ede1d63f281a0a2516"} {"question" "e-345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/2d81f8a2a521c0552b334dff813e37f4169d2221335ab71e391a2b0c686708ea.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA = xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $500\\,\\textrm{m}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+y = 500.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =500 \\\\ y & =500-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y = 500-2x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(500-2x) \\\\ A & =500x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $A'(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nA'(x) = 500 - 4x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 500-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =500 \\\\ x & =\\frac{500}{4} \\\\ x & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=125$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nA''(x) = -4 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad A''(125) = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\nSince $A''(125)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=125$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=125$, we have \n\n$$\ny=500-2(125)=250.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=125\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $y=250\\,\\textrm{m},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+y=375\\,\\textrm{m}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/example-345.json" "file_sha256" "d980a6047802007fac8163111e2d1e384673cbf2640e73ede1d63f281a0a2516"} {"question" "e-346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Among all rectangles with area $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2,$ what are the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/example-346.json" "file_sha256" "b1b97c448c2f595d7fd27ca32c7ca4fa210ce70d7618947d552cada0e05310dd"} {"question" "e-346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/a80e6f5620ba4e77487b675390fa04af778e538ecedbd55c783194cf3ab5772b.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP = 2(x + y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy = 25.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{25}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{25}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{50}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $P'(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 2 -\\frac{50}{x^2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{50}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =50 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nP''(x) = \\dfrac{100}{x^3} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad P''(5) = \\dfrac{100}{5^3} = \\dfrac 4 5 > 0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $P''(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{25}{5}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=5\\,\\textrm{m}$ and $y=5\\,\\textrm{m}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $5\\,\\textrm{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/example-346.json" "file_sha256" "b1b97c448c2f595d7fd27ca32c7ca4fa210ce70d7618947d552cada0e05310dd"}] "usage" {"e-345" [] "q-17810" [] "e-346" []} "basis" 2257 "retractions" [[17592186101172 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA builder has $320$ meters of fencing and she wants to enclose a rectangular parking lot. One side next to a building and so this side doesn't need to be fenced. Find $x + y$, where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the parking lot that has the greatest area."] [17592186100557 :question/problem "A farmer has $500$ meters of fencing and wants to fence off a rectangular field that borders a straight river. He needs no fence along the river. Find $x+y$, where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the field with the largest area that the farmer can fence off."] [17592186100557 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1211/Source/Images/0726404343df680759c61d2cfbac95e0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n$A = xy$\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $500\\,\\textrm{m}$. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+y = 500\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= 500 \\\\\ny &= 500 - 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y = 500-2x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= x(500 - 2x) \\\\\nA &= 500x - 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $A'(x)=0$. Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nA'(x) = 500 - 4x\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n500 - 4x &= 0 \\\\\n4x &= 500 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{500}{4} \\\\\nx &= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=125$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nA''(x) = -4 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad A''(125) = -4 < 0\n$$\n\nSince $A''(125)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=125$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x)$.\n\nFinally, when $x=125$, we have\n\n$$\ny=500-2(125)=250\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are\n\n$$\nx=125\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=250\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nand we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+y=375\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$"] [17592186100558 :question/problem "Among all rectangles with area $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2$, what are the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter?"] [17592186100558 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1211/Source/Images/701d1cff6dfcff15248651228352d0d2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP = 2(x + y)\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $25\\,\\textrm{m}^2$. This gives the equation\n$xy = 25$.\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{25}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= 2(x + \\frac{25}{x}) \\\\\n&= 2x + \\frac{50}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation $P'(x)=0$. Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 2 -\\frac{50}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n2 - \\frac{50}{x^{2}} &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} &= 50 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 25 \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nP''(x) = \\dfrac{100}{x^3} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad P''(5) = \\dfrac{100}{5^3} = \\dfrac 4 5 > 0\\,\n$$\n\nSince $P''(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x)$.\n\nFinally, when $x=5$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{25}{5} = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are\n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=5\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $5\\,\\textrm{m}$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186098799 17592186098800 17592186098801 17592186098802 17592186098803] "immutable_fields" [[17592186098798 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098798 :answer-field/id] [17592186098798 :answer-field/key] [17592186098798 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
